# =============================================================================
# Build-context exclusions.
#
# Everything not listed here is uploaded to the daemon on every build, so this
# file governs build speed as much as image contents. Two rules matter most:
#
#   1. Never ship a secret. A file excluded here cannot end up in a layer, and a
#      layer is forever - deleting a file in a later RUN does not remove it from
#      the image history.
#   2. Never ship host build output. A host node_modules carries darwin/glibc
#      binaries that break in an Alpine container, and a stale .next carries
#      generated route types pointing at paths that no longer exist - which
#      fails `next build` inside the image for reasons invisible in the source.
# =============================================================================

## Host build output. Must never enter the container - see rule 2 above.
## `.next*` also covers the NEXT_DIST_DIR convention (.next-prod, .next-dev).
node_modules
.next*
out
build
coverage
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

## Secrets. Belt and braces - .env is gitignored too, but a developer who has
## one locally must not have it baked into an image. Next loads .env during
## `next build`, so a stray copy would be inlined into the client bundle.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem

## Version control and IDE state.
.git
.gitignore
.vscode
.idea
.DS_Store

## Other deploy targets. Irrelevant to the container image.
netlify.toml
vercel.json
.vercel

## Documentation and generated analysis. Large, and changes on every doc edit,
## which would bust the build cache for no reason.
docs/
graphify-out/
*.md

## Docker's own files. Changing compose configuration must not invalidate the
## image build cache.
Dockerfile
Dockerfile.*
.dockerignore
compose*.yml
docker-compose*.yml

## Logs.
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*
